# Issue #1052: fmt breaks a long grammar rule inside a repetition bound `{2}`

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/1052).

`ready` · opened 2026-10-31 · updated 2026-10-31 · 0 comments

Found while writing `grammar_hex_digit_is_one_hex_digit.nika` (#1002).

```nika
grammar Pair {
	rule WS = "" -> { }
	entry rule top -> String = "#" r:hex_digit{2} g:hex_digit{2} b:hex_digit{2} eof -> { f"{r} {g} {b}" }
}
```

`nikaia fmt` turns the long line into

```nika
	entry rule top -> String = "#" r:hex_digit{
		2
	} g:hex_digit{2} b:hex_digit{2} eof -> { f"{r} {g} {b}" }
```

It still parses and runs the same, but the formatter reads the bound `{2}` as a block and breaks there. A bound is one token group and should never be broken; a long rule is broken between elements, or before `->`.

