# Issue #1124: A run throwing fn parameter beside an own throw: rustc finds no From<Box<dyn Error>> for Thrown<..>

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/1124).

`ready` · opened 2026-11-01 · updated 2026-11-01 · 0 comments

Found by the builder for #1120 on 2026-11-01. The defect is also in the binary from before that fix.

A function can have both of these:
- its own `throw Negative`,
- a parameter `fn(i64) -> i64 sync throws` that it runs.

When it does, the parameter's call `scoped(n)?` is lowered into the function's `Thrown<Negative>`. That conversion fails: rustc finds no `From<Box<dyn Error>>`.

It looks like the ADR-351 D4 throw set counts the errors of a code call as known, when they are an open `Box<dyn Error>`.

## Probe

```nika
struct Negative { why: String }
impl Error for Negative { fn message(ref self) -> String { return self.why.clone() } }

fn checked(n: i64, scoped: fn(i64) -> i64 sync throws) -> i64 sync throws {
	if n > 100 {
		throw Negative { why: "big" }
	}
	return scoped(n)
}

fn main() {
	let k: fn(i64) -> i64 sync throws = fn(x) {
		if x < 0 {
			throw Negative { why: "negative" }
		}
		return x * 2
	}
	let d = checked(21, fn(n) { return k(n) }) catch {
		println(f"refused: {error}")
		return
	}
	println(f"{d}")
}
```

Expected output: `42`. Actual: rustc refuses the program (`From<Box<dyn Error>>` is not implemented for `Thrown<Negative>`).

## To build
- [ ] When a function's throw set includes a call of code (a parameter, a kept value or a field), make the set open (`Box<dyn Error>`), or widen the `Thrown` enum by an open arm. Follow ADR-351 D4.
- [ ] Add a test in `tests/compiler/tests/` built from the probe, with its `.stdout`. Then add the throws case to `a_function_type_takes_a_function.nika` (#1120 left it out because of this defect).

