# Issue #1212: A `Shared`/`SharedMut` handle used after it was handed by value reaches rustc ("`Rc` does not implement `Copy`")

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/1212).

`ready` · opened 2026-11-03 · updated 2026-11-03 · 0 comments

Part I §6.2 says that handing a `Shared` or `SharedMut` by value duplicates the handle, not the data, and the compiler steps the owner count. Today the second handle is not made: the lowering moves the `Rc`, and the next use fails in rustc. A backend error reaching user code is a defect (§6.8).

Probe (`target/fast/nikaia run`):

```nika
struct Settings { name: String, retries: int }

fn show(s: Shared[Settings]) {
    println(s.name)
}

fn main() {
    let settings = Shared(Settings { name: "prod", retries: 3 })
    let for_log = settings
    show(for_log)
    println(settings.retries)
}
```

```text
error: The type `Rc` does not implement `Copy`.
   --> e2.nika:11:5
    = note: Consider using `Rc::clone`.
```

The same with `SharedMut`, which blocks every structure whose parts point at one another:

```nika
struct Node { name: String, next: SharedMut[Node]? }

fn main() {
    let a = SharedMut(Node { name: "a", next: null })
    let b = SharedMut(Node { name: "b", next: a })
    a.update fn(mut n) { n.next = b }
    println("linked")
}
```

`.clone()` on a handle is refused (`NK1171`), so there is no way to write it in the source either (and per §6.2 none should be needed).

Found while writing the ownership part of the syntax tour (`guide/syntax.md`), which marks handle sharing as not built and links here.

## To build

1. Lowering: a `Shared`/`SharedMut`/`Locked` handle that is handed by value (a `let`, an argument, a field, a list element) and used again afterwards is written as `Rc::clone`/`Arc::clone` of the handle; the last use moves it.
2. Tests in `tests/compiler/` for both probes, and for a handle handed to a task.
3. Decide and record how a cycle of handles (the second probe) is freed: today there is no weak handle, so an owner-count cycle is never released. Either a weak handle (a language question for the supervisor) or a documented limit in §6.2.

